{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 17,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.645,
            0.047,
            0.847,
            0.064
          ],
          "content": "A Peek Beyond the Point"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.088,
            0.848,
            0.199
          ],
          "content": "Thus decimal notation is a natural extension of the Indian place value system to numbers also having fractional parts. Just as 705 means $7 \\times 1 0 0 \\div 5 \\times 1$ , the number 70.5 means $7 \\times 1 0 \\pm 5 \\times \\frac { 1 } { 1 0 }$ , and 7.05 means $7 \\times 1 \\div 5 \\times \\frac { 1 } { 1 0 0 } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.203,
            0.848,
            0.241
          ],
          "content": "We have seen how to write numbers using the decimal point (‘.’). But how do we read/say these numbers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.251,
            0.713,
            0.27
          ],
          "content": "We know that 705 is read as **seven hundred and five**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.27,
            0.847,
            0.325
          ],
          "content": "70.5 is read as **seventy point five**, short for **seventy and five-tenths**. 7.05 is read as **seven point zero five**, short for **seven and five hundredths**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.325,
            0.848,
            0.381
          ],
          "content": "0.274 is read as **zero point two seven four**. We don’t read it as **zero point two hundred and seventy four** as 0.274 means **2 one-tenths and 7 one-hundredths and 4 one-thousandths**."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.39,
            0.846,
            0.43
          ],
          "content": "？ Make a place value table similar to the one above. Write each quantity in decimal form and in terms of place value, and read the number:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.181,
            0.445,
            0.57,
            0.465
          ],
          "content": "(a) 2 ones, 3 tenths and 5 hundredths"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.469,
            0.412,
            0.489
          ],
          "content": "(b) 1 ten and 5 tenths"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.493,
            0.48,
            0.513
          ],
          "content": "(c) 4 ones and 6 hundredths"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.517,
            0.574,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": "(d) 1 hundred, 1 one and 1 hundredth"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.543,
            0.344,
            0.579
          ],
          "content": "(e) $\\frac{8}{100}  and  \\frac{9}{10}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.584,
            0.271,
            0.62
          ],
          "content": "(f) $\\frac { 5 } { 1 0 0 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.625,
            0.26,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": "(g) $\\frac { 1 } { 1 0 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.182,
            0.664,
            0.458,
            0.701
          ],
          "content": "(h) $2\\frac{1}{100},4\\frac{1}{10}\\bmod7\\frac{7}{1000}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.703,
            0.846,
            0.739
          ],
          "content": "In the chapter on large numbers, we learned how to write 23 hundreds."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.74,
            0.607,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "23 hundred $s = 23 \\times 100 = 2000 + 300 = 2300.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.154,
            0.769,
            0.843,
            0.852
          ],
          "content": "| Thousands | Hundreds | Tens | Units |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | 23 |  |  |\n| 2 | 3 | 0 | 0 |",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.175,
                0.862,
                0.454,
                0.881
              ],
              "content": "Similarly, 23 tens would be:"
            },
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.176,
                0.881,
                0.506,
                0.899
              ],
              "content": "23 tens = 23 × 10 = 200 + 30 = 230."
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_17_table_17.jpg"
        },
